### What is the difference between transfer() and put()?

`put(e)` and `transfer(e)` both insert an element into the queue, but they differ in when the producer is unblocked:

- **put(e)**: the producer is unblocked as soon as the element is placed in the queue. The element may sit in the queue for a long time before a consumer reads it.
- **transfer(e)**: the producer is unblocked only when a consumer actually takes the element. The producer knows that its element has been received.

This makes `transfer()` a rendezvous point – a direct hand-off between producer and consumer.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class TransferQueueDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        TransferQueue<String> queue = new LinkedTransferQueue<>();

        // Consumer thread: takes elements
        Thread consumer = new Thread(() -> {
            try {
                Thread.sleep(500); // simulate delayed consumer
                String txn = queue.take();
                System.out.println("Consumer received: " + txn);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            }
        });
        consumer.start();

        // Producer: transfer blocks until consumer takes
        System.out.println("Producer: calling transfer (will block)...");
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        queue.transfer("TXN-001: High-value trade Rs.10Cr");
        long waited = (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1_000_000;
        System.out.println("Producer: transfer completed after " + waited + " ms");
        System.out.println("Producer: guaranteed consumer received the trade");

        consumer.join();
    }
}
```

```text
Producer: calling transfer (will block)...
Consumer received: TXN-001: High-value trade Rs.10Cr
Producer: transfer completed after 502 ms
Producer: guaranteed consumer received the trade
```

### How does tryTransfer work?

`tryTransfer(e)` attempts an immediate hand-off. If a consumer is already waiting (blocked in `take()`), the element is delivered and the method returns true. If no consumer is waiting, the method returns false immediately – the element is NOT queued.

`tryTransfer(e, timeout, unit)` waits up to the specified timeout for a consumer to arrive.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class TryTransferDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        TransferQueue<String> queue = new LinkedTransferQueue<>();

        // No consumer waiting
        boolean delivered = queue.tryTransfer("TXN-001");
        System.out.println("Immediate delivery (no consumer): " + delivered);
        System.out.println("Queue size: " + queue.size()); // 0 – NOT queued!

        // Start a consumer, then try transfer
        Thread consumer = new Thread(() -> {
            try {
                String item = queue.take(); // blocks waiting
                System.out.println("Consumer got: " + item);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            }
        });
        consumer.start();
        Thread.sleep(100); // let consumer start waiting

        delivered = queue.tryTransfer("TXN-002");
        System.out.println("Immediate delivery (consumer waiting): " + delivered);

        consumer.join();
    }
}
```

```text
Immediate delivery (no consumer): false
Queue size: 0
Consumer got: TXN-002
Immediate delivery (consumer waiting): true
```

### How is TransferQueue different from SynchronousQueue?

`SynchronousQueue` has zero capacity – every put blocks until a consumer takes. `LinkedTransferQueue` has a queue buffer. When you use `put()`, elements are queued normally. Only `transfer()` blocks for hand-off.

This makes LinkedTransferQueue more flexible: you can mix fire-and-forget (`put`) and guaranteed delivery (`transfer`) on the same queue.

| Feature | SynchronousQueue | LinkedTransferQueue |
|---|---|---|
| Capacity | 0 (no buffer) | Unbounded |
| put(e) | Blocks until take | Queues immediately |
| transfer(e) | N/A | Blocks until take |
| Flexibility | Only hand-off | Mix of queuing and hand-off |

### When should you use TransferQueue?

- **Request-response patterns.** Producer submits a task and needs confirmation that a worker received it before logging "task dispatched."
- **Guaranteed delivery.** In a banking system, the trade submission service uses `transfer()` to ensure the settlement engine has actually picked up the trade.
- **Adaptive behavior.** Use `tryTransfer()` to attempt immediate delivery; if no consumer is ready, fall back to `put()` for queued delivery.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class AdaptiveDeliveryDemo {
    private static final TransferQueue<String> queue = new LinkedTransferQueue<>();

    // Adaptive: try direct hand-off, fall back to queue
    public static void submit(String trade) throws InterruptedException {
        if (queue.tryTransfer(trade)) {
            System.out.println("Direct hand-off: " + trade);
        } else {
            queue.put(trade);
            System.out.println("Queued for later: " + trade);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        // No consumer yet – falls back to queue
        submit("TRD-001: HDFC Buy 100 shares");

        // Start consumer
        Thread consumer = new Thread(() -> {
            try {
                while (true) {
                    String trade = queue.take();
                    System.out.println("Processing: " + trade);
                    if (trade.contains("TRD-003")) break;
                }
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            }
        });
        consumer.start();
        Thread.sleep(100);

        // Consumer is waiting – direct hand-off
        submit("TRD-002: ICICI Sell 50 shares");
        Thread.sleep(100);
        submit("TRD-003: SBI Buy 200 shares");

        consumer.join();
    }
}
```

```text
Queued for later: TRD-001: HDFC Buy 100 shares
Processing: TRD-001: HDFC Buy 100 shares
Direct hand-off: TRD-002: ICICI Sell 50 shares
Processing: TRD-002: ICICI Sell 50 shares
Direct hand-off: TRD-003: SBI Buy 200 shares
Processing: TRD-003: SBI Buy 200 shares
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Lock-free implementation.** LinkedTransferQueue uses CAS (Compare-And-Swap) operations instead of locks, giving it excellent throughput under contention. It is one of the most efficient queue implementations in the JDK.

- **Unbounded only.** LinkedTransferQueue is always unbounded. There is no bounded version. If you need back-pressure, combine `tryTransfer` with fallback logic.

- **hasWaitingConsumer().** This method returns true if at least one consumer is blocked in `take()`. It can be used to make intelligent routing decisions.

- **Used in Executors.** Some custom executor implementations use LinkedTransferQueue for work dispatch because it can adapt between direct hand-off and queuing.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What is the difference between transfer() and put()?" – transfer blocks until a consumer receives; put queues and returns.
- "What happens if tryTransfer has no consumer?" – Returns false; element is NOT queued.
- "How is it different from SynchronousQueue?" – SynchronousQueue has zero capacity; LinkedTransferQueue has a buffer.
- "Is LinkedTransferQueue bounded?" – No, always unbounded.
